window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"10-13","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"10-13-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-01/","title_he":"א – שולחן של חסד","title_en":"A Table of Kindness","body_en":"<p>When a person sits down to eat his meal, he should think about the hungry poor and share his bread with them. And so we find that Avraham our father, peace be upon him, would seek out wayfarers in order to bring them into his home and feed them from the best of his foods (Bereishit 18), for he did not want to eat alone without needy guests. The Sages said: \"Hospitality to guests is greater than receiving the Divine Presence\" (Shabbat 127a; see Bava Metzia 86b).</p><p>Likewise we find in the Torah that whenever it speaks of the joy of the festival and its meals, or of eating the meat of the sacrifices and eating the holy fruits of ma'aser sheni and neta reva'i, we were commanded to share our joy also with the stranger, the orphan and the widow (Devarim 12:12; 14:29; 16:14).</p><p>The Sages said (Berakhot 54b; 55a) that one who lingers at his table, so that hungry poor people can join his meal, has his days and years lengthened. They further said: \"As long as the Temple stood, the altar atoned for Israel; and now, a person's table atones for him.\" For just as he revives the poor person, who is considered as dead, so too the Holy One, blessed be He, will revive him, as it is stated (Mishlei 10:2): \"And charity saves from death\" (Zohar vol. 3, 273b). And when there are hungry poor people who are liable to come to his table, and he leaves nothing for them, the Sages said of him (Sanhedrin 92a): \"Whoever does not leave bread on his table will never see a sign of blessing.\"</p><p>Today, it is true, poor people who are hungry for bread are not so common, but there are people who need to be treated with respect and affection, and it is a great mitzva to invite them to a meal, for great is the power of a meal to bring hearts closer and to gladden the downtrodden and the lonely. And the Sages said: Great is a sip (legima), that is, a meal at which one drinks wine that gladdens, for it brings close those who are distant (see Sanhedrin 103b).</p><p>One who has servants must take care that after they serve the foods, they too eat of all the choice foods. And if they serve a food whose aroma arouses a desire to eat it, one must give them a taste of it immediately, so that they will not suffer (see Ketubot 61a; SA 169:1; 170:3).</p><p>A person must also see to his animals before he eats, as the Sages said (Gittin 62a): \"A person is forbidden to taste anything until he gives food to his animal,\" as it is stated (Devarim 11:15): \"And I will give grass in your field for your cattle,\" and only afterwards is it stated, \"and you shall eat and be satisfied.\" Even to taste a little is forbidden if they are hungry, but to drink is permitted (Shevut Ya'akov 3:13; MB 167:40). All this applies to domesticated animals from which a person derives benefit, and which have no way of obtaining their food other than what they receive from their owners (She'elat Ya'avetz 1:17).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-02/","title_he":"ב – אורח","title_en":"A Guest","body_en":"<p>It is the custom of Israel that when a person happens to come to their home while they are eating, they invite him to join their meal, even if he is not poor. On the other hand, it is a good custom for the guest to decline a little at first, and only if he sees that they truly wish to invite him, for example if they ask him again or serve the food before him, should he join their meal (see Peninei Halakha: Likutim vol. 2, 3:4-5; 7:9-10).</p><p>A person is forbidden to derive benefit from \"a meal that is insufficient for its owner,\" that is, from a meal in which the owner does not want him to participate, because of avak gezel (a trace of robbery). And even if in practice they invited him to participate in the meal, if he knows that the host does not truly want him to participate in it because he is sparing of his money, it is considered \"a meal that is insufficient for its owner\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Teshuva 4:4). The Sages further said that a person is forbidden to derive benefit from the meal of a miser, and even if the miser has a personal motive to invite him, it is forbidden to participate in his meal, for \"whoever derives benefit from those who are begrudging transgresses a negative commandment\" (Sota 38b).</p><p>One who hosts his friend at his table must show him a cheerful face, so that he will eat with appetite and not worry that perhaps the host is distressed that his food is being finished. And especially he must show a cheerful face to the poor at his table.</p><p>It is fitting for a guest to show respect to the host and to listen to him, as the Sages said: \"Whatever the host tells you, do\" (Pesaḥim 86b). For example, if he asked him to lead the zimun, he should lead it. And if he asked him to say words of Torah, if he is able to say them, he should say them. And when he asks him to eat, he should eat. And one who is already satiated from the host's meal, and the host still urges him to eat more, is not obligated to continue eating, for since he has already eaten and been satiated from the host's meal, the host need not be embarrassed that he is not eating more (SA 170:5; MB there).</p><p>One who is accustomed to excessive stringency in matters of kashrut, when he is a guest, should eat from what the host offers him. But if he is accustomed to be stringent about something regarding which there is room for stringency even according to the rules of halakha, such as ḥalak meat, he need not be lenient at the host's request. However, there were rabbis among the great Torah scholars of Israel who, when they were guests, were accustomed to follow the lenient opinions, and would even eat kosher meat that was not ḥalak (glatt) (see MB 170:16; Piskei Teshuvot 8).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-03/","title_he":"ג – שלא להשחית מאכלים","title_en":"Not Destroying Food","body_en":"<p>One must be very careful not to destroy food, for whoever destroys even a kezayit of food transgresses the prohibition of bal tashḥit (wanton destruction) (Sifrei Devarim 20:19). Therefore the Sages warned not to pass a cup full of drink over bread, lest some of the liquid in the cup spill onto the bread and make it repulsive and unfit for eating. Likewise, it is forbidden to rest a utensil that may be dirty against bread, so as not to make the bread repulsive (Berakhot 40b; SA OḤ 171:1).</p><p>Likewise, it is forbidden to throw to one's friend foods that are liable to become repulsive if they fall to the ground, such as dates and figs. But foods that will not become repulsive if they fall, such as nuts, may be thrown (SA OḤ 171:1). One should not sit on foods that will be squashed by his sitting, lest they become repulsive. But if the foods are placed in a hard box, it is permitted to sit on the box (ibid. 2).</p><p>One who takes food onto his plate must be careful not to take too much, lest he be unable to finish eating it, and it turns out that he will cause the destruction of the food that remains on his plate. However, if he took too much and he feels that he is already satiated, there is no mitzva, nor even a measure of piety, to finish the food on his plate. This is because the health of his body is more important, and since excessive eating harms health, it is more important to be careful about bal tashḥit regarding his body than to be concerned about bal tashḥit regarding the food that will be thrown away (Shabbat 140b; and see below halakha 9, and Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 13:10).</p><p>It is permitted to use bread as a spoon in order to eat something else, provided that in the end he eats the bread. Those who are meticulous are careful to eat a little of the bread each time as well, so that it will not be degraded by being considered merely a spoon (SA 171:3).</p><p>One who sees bread or another food in a place where people walk on it and it is being degraded should, out of respect for it, lift it onto a rock or place it at the side of the path in a way that it will not be degraded by being walked on (Eruvin 64b; Beit Barukh 45:31).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-04/","title_he":"ד – שיירי המאכלים","title_en":"Leftover Food","body_en":"<p>One should make an effort not to destroy leftover food, and as long as it is still possible, one should serve it at the next meals or prepare another dish from it, such as a pashtida (baked casserole). Likewise, one who holds a large meal should preferably plan from the outset what to do with the leftover food, whether to freeze it in order to eat it later or to distribute it to friends or to the needy. And when there is no such possibility, because it is not so fit for eating, and the effort of keeping it outweighs its value, it is a pious custom to feed it to animals. And one who has a large garden can place it in the garden so that it will return to the earth and fertilize the soil (see Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 13:10).</p><p>When it is difficult to use the leftover food, one may place it in the garbage bin, and it is proper to wrap the bread so that it will not be degraded. Some wish to be stringent with themselves not to put leftover bread in the garbage, and they place the leftover bread next to the garbage bins and on the fences of houses. But they are mistaken, for in this way they degrade the bread publicly, cause distress to the neighbors and make the public domain unsightly. Therefore, when it is a large quantity, which the birds and animals cannot possibly eat in a short time, it is proper to wrap the leftover bread and place it inside the garbage bin.</p><p>If crumbs smaller than a kezayit remain after eating, according to the letter of the law there is no prohibition against discarding them and treating them disrespectfully; but the Sages said that it is not advisable to do so, because one who destroys and degrades bread crumbs brings poverty upon himself (SA 180:4). There are two reasons for this. First, one who throws crumbs on the ground appears to be kicking at the goodness that the Holy One, blessed be He, bestowed upon the world, and thereby he causes Heaven to stop bestowing further goodness upon him. Furthermore, there is a segula (spiritual remedy) in the matter: the angel appointed over food and livelihood is named \"Nakid,\" that is, his name is \"cleanliness,\" whereas the angel appointed over poverty is named \"Naval,\" that is, his name is \"filth.\" Therefore, in a place where there are food crumbs on the floor the angel of poverty dwells, whereas in a clean place the angel of wealth dwells (Pesaḥim 111b; Ḥullin 105b).</p><p>Therefore, it is proper to be careful not to leave scattered on the ground crumbs that recognizably have the form of bread, and certainly not to throw such crumbs from the table onto the floor, for then they are degraded when people tread on them. Rather, one should gather them from the floor and throw them into the yard or into water, or place them in the garbage bin respectfully (MB 180:10; see Peninei Halakha: Kashrut 13:12).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-05/","title_he":"ה – בל תשקצו באכילה","title_en":"Bal Teshaktzu (Not Making Oneself Abominable) in Eating","body_en":"<p>A person must guard his dignity, for he was created in the image of God, and therefore he should be careful not to do repulsive acts that degrade him. And so we were commanded regarding the prohibition of eating creeping creatures (sheratzim): in addition to the fact that it is forbidden to eat them, since they are among the impure creatures forbidden for eating, there is also an aspect of repulsiveness in eating them, as it is stated (Vayikra 11:43-44): \"Do not make yourselves abominable with any creeping thing that creeps, and do not make yourselves impure with them, that you should be defiled by them. For I am the Lord your God; sanctify yourselves therefore and be holy, for I am holy; and do not defile yourselves with any creeping thing that moves upon the earth.\"</p><p>Likewise, it is forbidden for a person to make himself abominable by eating filthy foods or drinks, for example ones into which vomit, excrement or putrid phlegm has become mixed. Likewise, it is forbidden for a person to eat from filthy vessels, such as vessels designated for excrement and urine. Likewise, it is forbidden for a person to eat with filthy hands. Whoever does one of these things transgresses the prohibition of bal teshaktzu. In principle, if there were witnesses who warned him not to do so and he did it, he may be punished with makat mardut (rabbinic lashes for disobedience) (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:29-30).</p><p>Just as it is forbidden for a person to eat disgusting foods, so too it is forbidden for him to do disgusting acts. Included in the prohibition of bal teshaktzu is not to hold back from relieving oneself, as will be explained below (halakha 13).<sup>1</sup></p><p>A food that is repulsive to most people, but that some like to eat: one to whom it is repulsive is forbidden to eat it, and one to whom it is not repulsive is permitted to eat it. But something from which all people recoil is forbidden to be eaten even by one who does not recoil from it, for his view is nullified in relation to the view of all people (Pri Ḥadash YD 84:3).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"According to the Yere'im, the Semak and the Ramah, all repulsive things are forbidden by Torah law because of bal teshaktzu, but one does not receive lashes for them (since it is a general negative commandment, lav she-bikhlalot), and the Sages instituted that he be given lashes. According to the Rambam, the Ritva, the Mishneh La-melekh there, the Levush and the Pri Ḥadash, one transgresses bal teshaktzu by Torah law only when eating creeping creatures, and for other repulsive things the prohibition is rabbinic (see Encyclopedia Talmudit vol. 3)."}]},{"slug":"10-13-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-06/","title_he":"ו – שלא להגעיל אנשים אחרים","title_en":"Not Disgusting Other People","body_en":"<p>When the repulsive act is done in front of people, there is an additional severity in it, for in addition to the fact that the one who does it degrades himself and makes himself abominable, he also causes his companions distress by causing them a feeling of disgust. He thereby transgresses the prohibition \"You shall not wrong one another\" (Vayikra 25:17), and neglects the positive commandment \"You shall love your neighbor as yourself\" (Vayikra 19:18).</p><p>There are acts that, when a person does them in private, there is no flaw in them, but in front of other people they are considered disgusting and are forbidden because of bal teshaktzu and because of the mitzvot between one person and another, as for example one who picks his nose or scratches pimples on his face. And so the Sages said (Ḥagiga 5a): \"'For God shall bring every work into judgment, concerning every hidden thing' (Kohelet 12:14). Rav said: This is one who kills a louse in front of his friend, and his friend is disgusted by it. And Shmuel said: This is one who spits in front of his friend, and his friend is disgusted by it.\" Especially while eating, one must be more careful about this, for repulsive acts, as well as disgusting talk, spoil the appetite and arouse nausea among those eating.</p><p>A person should not bite into a food and, when he sees that it does not taste good to him, take it out of his mouth and place it on the table, for thereby he disgusts those eating (SA 170:10). Likewise, a person should not gather crumbs that fell on the floor and became repulsive and place them on the table, for the onlookers are disturbed by this, worrying that perhaps he will eat them. Likewise, one who has begun to bite into a slice should not return it to the central basket or give it to his friend, for many are disgusted by this (SA 170:15; MB 36). Therefore, one who does not intend to eat a whole slice should cut off what he wishes to eat and leave the rest in the bread basket.</p><p>Likewise, one who has begun to drink from his cup should not afterwards give it to his friend so that he may continue drinking from it, lest his friend be disgusted by this; furthermore, eating and drinking from mouth to mouth may transmit diseases (see SA 170:16; MB 37). However, if his friend is not disgusted by this, he is permitted to drink from his friend's cup, and he is likewise permitted to eat his friend's leftover food. And this is accepted among family members.</p><p>There are acts that were accepted in the past and today are impolite, and one must behave according to what is accepted in our day. For example, in the past people were accustomed to eat with their hands, and today it is customary to eat with cutlery, and one who eats with his hands transgresses a prohibition, for he causes a feeling of disgust in his companions and degrades himself. Likewise, in the past it was accepted to spit on the floor of the house and in front of people (see Berakhot 63a). And the physicians held that anyone who smells the aroma of a food that arouses in him a great craving and cannot eat of it should immediately spit out the saliva that has accumulated in his mouth, for otherwise he will endanger his life (Ketubot 61b). Today one should not do so, for this is not considered dangerous, and it is also not polite to spit in front of others.</p><p>There are things that are considered polite in one society, while in the eyes of others they are considered disgusting, and a person should become familiar with the various customs, so that he can behave sensitively and not cause a feeling of disgust to one whose customs are different. For example, some are accustomed to dip their bread into the central plate of hummus, and some are disgusted by this. When all those sitting there are accustomed to dip their bread into the central salad, there is no prohibition in this. But if there is someone there who is disgusted by it, they must refrain from it.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-07/","title_he":"ז – נימוסים בסעודה","title_en":"Etiquette at a Meal","body_en":"<p>When several people sit at a meal, each of those eating should take his equal share from the central bowl, and even if his friend took only a little at first, he should take his equal share, for perhaps his friend will later want to eat more. Only if he sees that his friend is not interested in eating more should he ask to take what remains (see SA 170:2).</p><p>When there is among those eating an eminent person or the father of the family, they should wait until he first takes his share from the central bowl, and only afterwards should the others take their share (SA 170:12).</p><p>It is not proper to gaze intently at the face of one who is eating, so that he will not be embarrassed. Especially, the host must not look at the face of the guest while he is eating or while he is taking food onto his plate (SA 170:4). Likewise, one should not photograph a person while he is eating without his consent.</p><p>One should not put a large amount of food into his mouth at once, in the manner of gluttons. Likewise, one should not drink a whole cup of wine or another alcoholic drink in one go (SA 170:7-8; MB 22). One should not eat quickly, nor take noisy bites of food, but should eat slowly with his mouth closed. And he should be careful that his clothes, hands and beard do not become soiled (Ben Ish Ḥai, Behar 8-9).</p><p>The refined people (nekiyei ha-da'at) of Jerusalem would not enter a meal unless they first knew who would be dining with them (Sanhedrin 23a; SA 170:20). This is because it is unbecoming for a Torah scholar to sit with frivolous people at a meal, especially at a meal with wine and strong drinks, which open hearts and bring those eating closer to one another. Some are lenient regarding a se'udat mitzva (a meal celebrating a mitzva), for the merit of the mitzva will help ensure that there will be no frivolity in their gathering. And if it is known that they will not behave with seriousness, even at a se'udat mitzva one should not drink wine and strong drinks in their company. And when it is a respected Torah scholar regarding whom it is known that they will behave in his company with proper respect, even though in other places they behave frivolously, he is permitted to dine and drink with them at the celebration of a mitzva, for through this they will draw closer to Torah and mitzvot (see Be'ur Halakha there).</p><p>One who has young children participating in the meal must supervise them so that they do not put their hands into the main bowl and make the food repulsive to the other diners. He should also take care that they do not become too dirty and that their noses are clean. And if there is a guest there, they should not seat next to him, without his wishing it, young children who tend to get dirty (Sefer Ḥasidim 829; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 170:78).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-08/","title_he":"ח – דברי תורה ודיבורים בסעודה","title_en":"Words of Torah and Conversation at a Meal","body_en":"<p>\"Rabbi Shimon says: Three who ate at one table and did not say words of Torah over it, it is as if they ate of offerings to the dead, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 28:8): 'For all tables are full of vomit and filth, without the Omnipresent.' But three who ate at one table and said words of Torah over it, it is as if they ate from the table of the Omnipresent, blessed be He, as it is stated (Yeḥezkel 41:22): 'And he said to me: This is the table that is before the Lord'\" (Avot 3:3). The main obligation to say words of Torah is when three eat together, for then their meal is significant, and corresponding to its physical significance, one must give significance to the spiritual aspect through words of Torah. For otherwise, the meal is like a dead body whose soul has been taken from it, and consequently the foods served at it are like offerings to the dead. But when words of Torah are said at the meal, the soul becomes bound to the body and bestows life and blessing upon it.</p><p>When there are not three eating together, they are not obligated to say words of Torah, for Birkat Ha-mazon suffices for them (Magen Avot of the Rashbatz). Some say that it is preferable that even an individual who eats should engage a little in Torah (MB 170:1).</p><p>Saying Shir Ha-ma'alot or other verses can also be considered words of Torah (MB 170:1). But it is better to say words of Torah that have value and add understanding, so that through them the entire meal will be elevated.</p><p>The Sages said (Ta'anit 5b): One does not converse during a meal, lest the windpipe precede the esophagus and he choke (SA 170:1). For since he is speaking, the windpipe (the breathing tube) is open and the food may enter it. Today many are lenient about this. Some explained that the prohibition applied when people ate reclining, that is, half lying down, but when eating while sitting there is no danger in this (Perisha; Birkei Yosef). And some hold that even today one must be stringent about this (Pri Megadim, Eshel Avraham 1; and see Arukh Ha-shulḥan 2). In any event, even one who is lenient should be careful about etiquette, that his speech be pleasant, that the diners not see the food inside his mouth, and all the more so that remnants of food not spray from his mouth while he speaks.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-09/","title_he":"ט – שלא לאכול יותר מדי","title_en":"Not Eating Too Much","body_en":"<p>A person is forbidden to indulge in excessive eating. There are two aspects to the prohibition: first, there is a health risk in it; second, there is a moral flaw in it, in that a person is drawn excessively after physical desire.</p><p>Regarding the health risk in eating a great deal, the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot De'ot 4:2): \"A person should not eat until his stomach is full; rather, he should eat about a quarter less than his satiety.\" And after the Rambam detailed which foods are harmful to health, he added and wrote (ibid. 4:14-15): \"As long as a person exercises and exerts himself a great deal, and does not eat to satiety, and his bowels are loose, no illness comes upon him and his strength increases, even if he eats bad foods… And overeating is like poison to the body of every person, and it is the root of all illnesses. Most of the illnesses that come upon a person are only either because of bad foods, or because he fills his stomach and overeats, even of good foods.\" And this is also agreed upon by the medical experts of our day, that excessive eating is very harmful to health.</p><p>In addition, a person is forbidden to be drawn excessively after physical desire. The Ramban explained that this is what the Torah intended in the command \"You shall be holy\" (Vayikra 19:2). For if the Torah had not commanded us to be holy, a person could be \"a scoundrel with the Torah's permission,\" gorging on meat without limit and drinking a great deal of wine and strong drinks, and claiming that he has no sin, since the meat and wine are kosher of the highest standard. Therefore we were commanded \"You shall be holy,\" so that one should not overeat.</p><p>The Torah did not set a measure for overeating, because people's needs differ from one another: some have large bodies and some have small bodies; some have a fast metabolism and need more food, and some have a slow metabolism and need less food. And some have the same physical characteristics, but one works at hard physical labor and requires a great deal of food, while his friend works at light labor and needs less food (Shela, Ma'amar Shevi'i 12).</p><p>On the other hand, a person must be careful not to cause himself suffering by abstaining from good foods, as is cited in the Talmud in many places that, from a certain aspect, the nazirite who abstains from wine is called a sinner, and therefore it is stated (Bamidbar 6:11): \"And he shall make atonement for him, for having sinned regarding the soul.\" And Rabbi Elazar Ha-Kappar said: \"Is this not an a fortiori argument? If this one, who caused himself suffering only by abstaining from wine, is called a sinner, one who causes himself suffering by abstaining from each and every thing, all the more so.\" From here Shmuel learned: \"Whoever sits in fasting is called a sinner\" (Ta'anit 11a; Nedarim 10a; Nazir 19a; 22a; Bava Kamma 91b). And although there are other opinions, this is the main position: only for the sake of repentance or in special cases is there room for fasting and abstinence, but in general it is fitting for a person to enjoy all the good and varied foods that the Holy One, blessed be He, created in the world, and to thank God for them. Through this the sanctification of God's name will increase in the world, both through the blessings he recites to God, and through the good feeling that stems from the tasty foods he eats, as a result of which he will be able to look upon the Torah and the world with a good eye, and he will be happy and good to people. And so Rava and Rav Naḥman said about themselves, that in the merit of eating meat and drinking wine their eyes were opened to understand the Torah more (see Yoma 76b; Bava Kamma 71b). And so the Sages said (Yerushalmi Kiddushin 4:12) that a person will in the future have to give an accounting for everything his eye saw and he did not eat. But if he goes to excess in eating, he will sink into the swamp of materialism, and will not be able to direct his heart to the spirit and the soul, and in the end he will also be sick, sad and depressed.</p><p>A good piece of advice for preventing excessive eating: toward the end of the meal, take a break of about twenty minutes. Only after that, if he has a need, should he continue eating. For often, while a person is eating, his appetite continues and he thinks that he has not yet eaten as much as he needs. But if he stops for some time, his body will begin to digest what he ate, and consequently he will feel a sense of satiety. One can use this break for Torah study at the table, or one can recite Birkat Ha-mazon and go to various activities, and if afterwards he wishes to continue eating, he can return and eat more with a blessing.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-10/","title_he":"י – המאכלים הבריאים והמאכלים המזיקים","title_en":"Healthy Foods and Harmful Foods","body_en":"<p>There is a mitzva in maintaining a healthy lifestyle, and this is included in what the Torah taught (Devarim 28:9): \"And you shall walk in His ways.\" For God created our body to be healthy, and we must guard it and not eat foods that will impair its health. There is also in this a preparation for the fulfillment of all the mitzvot, for if he is healthy and balanced in his body and soul, he will be able to study Torah with clarity of mind, he will be able to be diligent and energetic in fulfilling the mitzvot, and he will be able to be joyful in his faith, at ease and good to people. But if he is sick, besides the fact that his days will be shortened and he will do less in the service of God, even while he lives he will be preoccupied with aches and pains, he will be frequently irritable and angry, he will find it difficult to delve into Torah and to see God's goodness, and he will not be able to turn his heart to the important challenges that the Creator designated for man. These guidelines too are included in what is stated (Mishlei 3:6): \"In all your ways know Him, and He will make your paths straight.\" And so the Sages said (Avot 2:12): \"And let all your deeds be for the sake of Heaven.\" And so it is told of Hillel the Elder that when he would go out to attend to the needs of his body, he would say that he was going out for the sake of a mitzva, since with his body he stands before God and fulfills His mitzvot (see Avot De-Rabbi Natan, version B, 30).</p><p>And so the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot De'ot 4:1): \"Since having a healthy and whole body is among the ways of God, for it is impossible to understand or know anything while one is sick, a person must distance himself from things that destroy the body and accustom himself to things that make one healthy and restore one's strength\" (and so too there 3:3).</p><p>In each and every generation the Sages recommended eating healthy foods according to what was known from the medical knowledge of those days. And it is reasonable that there are foods that were healthy in the past and today are not healthy, and there are foods that were not healthy in the past and today are healthy. For living conditions have changed considerably: foods that tended to spoil without a refrigerator, and whose consumption was dangerous, are considered healthy today, since they can be stored properly. And there are foods that in times of food shortage were considered very healthy, because of the fat and sugar in them, and in our day, when there is a surplus of food, they are very harmful.<sup>2</sup></p><p>Today the accepted guidance is to reduce as much as possible the consumption of processed foods, such as sugar, margarine, salt, white flour and saturated fat. Among the foods characterized by these ingredients are soft drinks, sausages, bourekas and cakes. On the other hand, it is recommended to eat plenty of vegetables and fruit, to drink water and to engage in physical activity. And above all, the most important thing is not to eat too much.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The Rambam, in Hilkhot De'ot chapter 4, mentions a list of good foods and good habits, and bad foods and bad habits, and they do not always coincide with the guidance of Ḥazal. For example, his guidance regarding bloodletting is different (see there, halakha 18), and the Kesef Mishneh explained that this is because the remedies of different places and times are not the same. Similarly wrote Tosafot (Mo'ed Katan 11a) and the Yam Shel Shlomo (Ḥullin 8:12). In the Yad Peshuta, in the introduction to chapter 4, he cited the words of Rav Sherira Gaon and Rabbi Avraham son of the Rambam, who explained that Ḥazal did not intend in their medical guidance to establish halakha, but rather to guide people to heed the advice of the medical experts of those days. And if the medical experts say otherwise, one must act in accordance with them. End of quotation. Therefore, although the Rambam was a great posek and an important physician, today we do not follow all his guidance, because we follow what is accepted by physicians in our day."}]},{"slug":"10-13-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-11/","title_he":"יא – האם יש איסור לאכול מאכלים מזיקים","title_en":"Is It Forbidden to Eat Harmful Foods?","body_en":"<p>Although one should make an effort to eat only healthy foods, there is no absolute prohibition in eating foods that are not healthy, and therefore one who eats them must recite a blessing over them. If it were forbidden to eat them, then according to what we have learned (above 12:10), it would be forbidden to recite a blessing over them, and we have not heard of anyone who claimed so. Even the Rambam (Hilkhot De'ot ch. 4), who detailed the bad foods that it is not proper to eat, did not write this as an absolute prohibition (see above 9:2, note 3; and likewise Igrot Moshe ḤM vol. 2, 76, and Nishmat Avraham ḤM 155:2, in the words of Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach).</p><p>This is because, apparently, health guidelines are not absolute, and it is reasonable that even foods that are considered bad are not harmful in a small quantity for one who eats little. Furthermore, there are poor people who cannot buy healthy foods, which are generally more expensive. If they deprive themselves of the bad foods, they will be harmed more, for they will become weak and fall ill from lack of food. Thus every food, even one that is not healthy, is beneficial in a certain situation and adds to health.</p><p>In addition, people differ from one another in their psychological and physical characteristics, and it is possible that these foods are harmful only to some people, but not to others. There are also other factors that affect health, and it is possible that only in combination with additional factors does that food cause great harm, and without their combination it is not so harmful. In addition, one's mood also affects health, and it is possible that one who has become accustomed to eating certain foods, if he abstains from them completely, will become so depressed that the depression itself will cause more serious illnesses in his body.</p><p>However, foods that cause danger within a short time are certainly forbidden to eat, and one who sins and eats them does not recite a blessing. For example, a dangerously ill person whom the doctors instructed to keep a strict diet, for otherwise there is a concern that he will die within a short time, is forbidden to eat the foods that are harmful to him.</p><p>But regarding other people, although one must make great efforts to eat healthy foods and to refrain from eating harmful foods, and it is also proper to educate the public toward healthy nutrition and a healthy lifestyle, nevertheless one should not do this with a heavy hand, since there is no absolute prohibition in this.</p><p>Likewise, one who has become excessively overweight, although he must make great efforts to lose weight, should preferably do so gently and gradually. For if he wages an all-out war against his body and his appetite, there is a concern that an extreme diet will cause more serious harm to his mental and physical health.</p><p>It should further be noted that from time to time new health methods are publicized, and each method has its own believers and devotees, who are certain that it alone is correct, and that whoever follows its guidelines will live in health and happiness and will have length of days. But after a number of years it becomes clear that although that method had correct aspects, it also had serious errors. Therefore, the correct guidance according to halakha is to follow the guidance of the majority of doctors (see SA OḤ 618). And one who feels that a certain method is good for him may follow its guidelines, provided that it does not contradict the position accepted by the majority of expert doctors. And on an additional condition: that he not follow it in an extreme manner, for extremism too may be dangerous.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-12/","title_he":"יב – הפרשת הפסולת מהגוף וברכת 'אשר יצר'","title_en":"Eliminating Waste from the Body and the Blessing of Asher Yatzar","body_en":"<p>After a person has eaten and drunk, he digests into his body the choice part of the food, from which he receives strength and vitality, whereas the superfluous part, the waste, he excretes. When a person has difficulty with this, his suffering is great and his whole body becomes ill. And if no cure is found for his illness, he will die in agony. Therefore the Sages instituted a special blessing of praise and thanksgiving to God for the elimination of waste from the body. Within it we give thanks for the creation of the whole body, which was made with wisdom: that God created organs that have hollow spaces, such as the heart, lungs, stomach and intestines, and created openings for the body, the nose for breathing and the mouth for eating and drinking, and He also created a special system for eliminating liquid and solid waste from the body. And if one detail of this wondrous system stops functioning, the person cannot survive. And most wondrous of all is how the Creator bound the spiritual soul to the physical body, so that when the body is sick, the soul cannot find expression, and when the body dies, the soul departs.</p><p>Generally a person is occupied with what he wishes to obtain — foods, drinks, property — whereas this action of eliminating waste from the body does not, in his opinion, deserve special attention. Only after he becomes ill does he understand how much he ought to thank God for it. Therefore, it is proper for a person, while he is still healthy, to have proper intention in the blessing of Asher Yatzar, and perhaps through this he will merit good health and length of days.</p><p>The Sages spoke highly of the value of the bathroom. Rabbi Yosi said: \"Who is rich? Anyone who has a bathroom near his table\" (Shabbat 25b). And according to Mar Zutra, every pious person must pray regarding the matter of the bathroom (Berakhot 8a).</p><p>There is a great secret in this. Many think that a person's troubles stem only from what he lacks, but the truth is that a large part of the harm that befalls a person stems from the superfluous things that accumulate in his mind, in his soul and in his body, for if he cannot rid himself of them, he gradually degenerates. When a person studies, within all the study material he absorbs there are also false ideas; and there are ideas that in themselves contain truth, but they are not suited to his personality, and he must find the way to separate them from his mind. Likewise with regard to emotional experiences: there are experiences which, if they accumulate in his soul, will weigh upon him until they drive him from the world, and he must separate them from his soul. Superfluous property too can weigh upon a person, until instead of helping him fulfill his destiny, he becomes a slave to his property and his desires, and loses his world. It turns out, then, that one of the most important actions for a person is the elimination of the waste that accumulates in his body, his soul, his property and his intellect, in order to enable him to continue living a proper and healthy life.</p><p>In the blessing of Asher Yatzar, we thank and praise God for having created us with wondrous wisdom, and having given us vessels and openings, through which we can take in from outside things that benefit us, and expel what is superfluous and harmful.</p><p>From this one can understand the distance we keep from excrement and urine, to the point that it is forbidden to say any sacred matter (davar she-bi-kedusha) near them (Peninei Halakha: Prayer 3:9). For there is in them a certain aspect of the impurity of the dead, for they are dead waste, remnants of food that cannot add life, and the body must separate them from itself so that they will not harm it.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-13/","title_he":"יג – בל תשקצו בהשהיית צרכיו","title_en":"Bal Teshaktzu in Delaying Relieving Oneself","body_en":"<p>A person who needs to relieve himself should do so promptly, in order to cleanse himself of the waste in his body. And if he needs to so much that he must hold himself in to prevent the waste from leaving his body, and the need to relieve himself is so pressing that he cannot take his mind off it, and nevertheless he does not go to relieve himself, he transgresses the prohibition of bal teshaktzu (Makkot 16b; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Mahadura Tinyana 3:11; Eshel Avraham of Buczacz). In addition, one who holds back from relieving himself harms his health (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 17:31). And a man who holds back from urinating endangers himself, even to the point of infertility (see Yevamot 64b; MB 3:31).</p><p>And if he can wait longer than the time it takes to walk a parsa (72 minutes), although it is desirable that he go to relieve himself, there is no obligation to do so, and he is permitted to study Torah and recite blessings during that time (MB 92:7). And if he began to study or to engage in his affairs when he did not need to relieve himself, and after a while he came to need to relieve himself to the point that he cannot hold out for 72 minutes, but he still does not need to hold himself in to keep the waste from leaving his body: according to most poskim, ideally he should stop and go to relieve himself. But if he is in the middle of a matter, he is permitted to delay relieving himself a little, until he finishes the matter (Be'ur Halakha 92:2, s.v. \"kore\"), and some say that even in the middle of a matter it is preferable to stop immediately in order to relieve himself (Ben Ish Ḥai, Vayetze 1). However, even one who needs to very much, if going out to relieve himself will cause him great embarrassment, according to all opinions he is permitted to wait longer, for great is human dignity (kevod ha-beriyot), which overrides the prohibition of bal teshaktzu (MB 3:31; 92:7; Peninei Halakha: Prayer 5:9).</p><p>One should conduct himself modestly in the bathroom, and not uncover himself beyond what is needed so as not to become soiled. After he relieves himself, he should clean himself well, and when necessary it is good that he clean himself with water or with wet paper (SA 3:4; MB 31). He should also be very careful not to soil the toilet, and if by mistake he soiled it a little, he should clean up after himself, so as not to cause distress to those who come to relieve themselves after him.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"10-13-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-14/","title_he":"יד – הלכות ברכת 'אשר יצר'","title_en":"The Laws of the Blessing of Asher Yatzar","body_en":"<p>After relieving himself, one should wash his hands and recite Asher Yatzar. There is no difference whether he defecated or urinated, and even if only a few drops came out of him, he must recite Asher Yatzar, for if those drops had accumulated in his body without being able to come out, he would have been harmed (SA 7:4). And even if the passing of urine or feces is accompanied by great pain, he should recite Asher Yatzar for having succeeded in relieving himself (Shulḥan Gavoha 7:3).<sup>3</sup></p><p>One who suffers from diarrhea and needs to relieve himself many times must recite Asher Yatzar after each and every time. But if, immediately after he finished relieving himself, he feels that he will soon need to relieve himself again, he should recite the blessing after he relieves himself the second time (MB 7:2).</p><p>Since it was ruled in halakha that a person who relieved himself does not recite the blessing \"Al Netilat Yadayim\" over washing his hands, one who leaves the bathroom does not need to wash his hands with a washing cup (natla); rather, it suffices to rinse them once with water from the tap. Some are meticulous to wash their hands with a vessel. And some are meticulous to rinse each hand three times alternately. From the standpoint of derekh eretz, for the sake of cleanliness and health, it is proper to wash with soap.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Even one who requires a catheter inserted into the bladder in order to pass urine should recite Asher Yatzar after the passing of urine (Har Tzvi OḤ 1:6). If the catheter has a stopper that is opened every few hours, whereby the urine comes out, he should recite the blessing each time after opening it. And if it is connected to a bag into which the urine flows constantly, he should recite Asher Yatzar once a day as part of Birkhot Ha-shaḥar, and have in mind with it everything he will pass during the day (Nishmat Avraham OḤ 7, in the name of Rabbi Shlomo Zalman Auerbach; Tzitz Eliezer 18:78:4)."},{"n":4,"en":"The Rishonim disagree whether the Sages instituted the blessing \"Al Netilat Yadayim\" after using the bathroom. According to the Ra'avad, one must recite it even after urinating. According to the father of the Agur, one must recite it after defecating. According to the Mordekhai, if he is about to study or pray he must recite Al Netilat Yadayim. According to the Rosh, only if he is about to pray and his hands touched a soiled place must he recite Al Netilat Yadayim. According to the Rashba, they instituted the recitation of Al Netilat Yadayim only in the morning and before a meal, and so too wrote the Mahari Abuhav in the name of Rabbeinu Tam, and this is the custom, and so it was ruled in halakha (BY and SA 7:1). And since one does not recite the blessing, a washing cup is not needed. But there is an enhancement (hiddur) in using a washing cup, because according to the Rishonim who hold that one must recite Al Netilat Yadayim, their enactment would in any case require washing with a washing cup (for the wording of the blessing was instituted with reference to the washing vessel; see Rosh on Berakhot 9:23). The book Heikhal Ha-kodesh was stringent to wash three times, and MA 7:1 rejected his words. So too wrote MB 4:39, and this is the opinion of most poskim. And the Ben Ish Ḥai (Toledot 16) wrote to rinse three times and did not require a washing cup. <b>Until when may he recite the blessing?</b> From the SA 7:3 it is implied that he may recite it the entire day; moreover, if he forgot and has already relieved himself a second time, he should recite Asher Yatzar twice. In this most poskim disagree with him, holding that one does not recite Asher Yatzar twice. But as long as he has not needed to relieve himself again, he should make up his blessing. And if he already feels the urge to relieve himself again, he should recite Asher Yatzar after he relieves himself the second time (MA; MB 7:6; Sha'ar Ha-tziyun 4; and this is the opinion of the poskim of Ashkenazic descent and some of the poskim of Sephardic descent). According to the Ben Ish Ḥai (Vayetze 12), one may recite the blessing only up to half an hour. And according to Yeḥaveh Da'at 4:5, one may recite the blessing up to 72 minutes. The Aḥaronim disagree <b>whether one may wash one's hands at the sink in the bathroom</b>. Some are lenient (Eretz Tzvi 111). And some are stringent (Har Tzvi 1:50). In practice, in a pressing situation one may be lenient (Minḥat Yitzḥak 1:60). And if it is a combined toilet and shower room, when necessary one may be lenient. Ideally, even in a combined toilet and shower room it is preferable not to wash one's hands there. (And see Yabia Omer 3:2, that a tzurat ha-petaḥ (doorway-form) can be effective in a bathroom.)"}]},{"slug":"10-13-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/10-13-15/","title_he":"טו – ברכת הרפואה","title_en":"The Blessing over Healing","body_en":"<p>Sometimes a person's body becomes ill and he needs to attend to his healing, and he should know that the root of the illness lies in a spiritual deficiency, caused by a sin or because he needed to ascend to a higher level, and since he did not ascend, suffering came upon him to purify and elevate him. Therefore, together with the fact that he must go to a doctor and make use of medications in order to recover by natural means, he must make a spiritual accounting (ḥeshbon nefesh), in order to examine the root of his illness, as the Sages said (Berakhot 5a): \"If a person sees that suffering is coming upon him, he should examine his deeds.\" And this was the sin of King Asa, that even after he fell ill because of his sins, he sent to seek out doctors, and did not repent and examine his deeds, as it is stated (II Divrei Ha-yamim 16:12): \"Yet in his illness he did not seek the Lord, but the physicians.\"</p><p>Even when the doctors are certain that a particular treatment will help the patient, there is still doubt in the matter, both because the human body is complex and the doctors cannot grasp all the processes that take place in it, and because the root of the illness lies in the soul. Therefore, without help from Heaven no treatment will be effective, and this is why the Sages instituted that one pray to God that the medical treatment succeed, and through this we will be roused to repentance.</p><p>Therefore, a patient entering medical treatment should say before entering: \"Yehi ratzon milfanekha Hashem Elohai she-yehei esek zeh li li-refua, ki rofei ḥinam Ata\" (\"May it be Your will, Lord my God, that this undertaking be for my healing, for You are a Healer who heals freely\"). And a healthy person who wishes to be meticulous and say this also before treatment for an ailment from which he suffers, or before taking a pill for a headache and the like, may blessing come upon him.</p><p>After the treatment, if it involved danger to life, such as a catheterization, a stroke or surgery under general anesthesia, one recites: \"Barukh Ata Hashem Eloheinu Melekh ha-olam rofei ḥolim\" (\"Blessed are You, Lord our God, King of the universe, who heals the sick\"). And after a treatment that did not involve danger, one says: \"Barukh rofei ḥolim\" (\"Blessed is He who heals the sick\") (our master Rav Kook). The custom of those of Sephardic descent is that even after a treatment involving danger, one says the blessing without God's name and kingship (shem u-malkhut), but one who wishes to rely on our master Rav Kook may do so.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The source of the blessing is in Berakhot 60a, regarding bloodletting for medical purposes. The Siddur of Rav Sa'adia Gaon, Sefer Ha-ora of Rashi, the Semag, the Semak, the Ra'avan and Orḥot Ḥayim wrote to say the blessing with God's name and kingship. And so it is in the common printed editions of the Rambam, and so it is cited in the BY 230:4 as halakha. So too wrote Rabbi David Abudarham, who explained that included in bloodletting are other treatments as well that involve cutting veins. And so too wrote to recite the blessing with God's name and kingship: the Levush, the Perisha, Kitzur Ha-Shela, the Taz 3, Ḥayei Adam 65:1, and Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 61:4. It is true that many Rishonim mentioned the blessing without God's name and kingship, but this is how blessings are generally written, as the BY wrote there; but there is no Rishon who wrote explicitly not to say it with God's name and kingship. And some Aḥaronim wrote that it is not customary to say the blessing with God's name and kingship (Pri Megadim, Eshel Avraham 6; Eshel Avraham of Buczacz there; MB 7). In addition, according to the precise versions of the Rambam, it emerges that one should not say this blessing with God's name and kingship, and some hold that had the BY seen the Rambam, he would have retracted, since he cites the Rambam as the source of the blessing. But this is not necessarily so, because many Rishonim wrote to say this blessing with God's name and kingship. In practice, most of the later Sephardic poskim wrote not to say this blessing with God's name and kingship. So too wrote Rabbi Eliyahu in his siddur, and Birkat Hashem vol. 4, 7:19. And our master Rav Kook ruled that when the medical treatment involves danger to life, one must recite the blessing with God's name and kingship, since it is clear that its law is at least like that of bloodletting. But if the medical procedure involves no concern of danger, one should give thanks without mentioning God's name and kingship (Olat Re'iya vol. 1, p. 390). And although the prevalent custom among those of Sephardic descent is not to recite the blessing, one who wishes to recite it may rely on our master Rav Kook, of blessed memory, and this is not a violation of the custom, for so wrote the BY, and so ruled Rabbi Yitzḥak Bueno in Shulḥan Melakhim 230:5. Those who immigrated from Yemen may also recite the blessing, for so ruled the Shetilei Zeitim 230:7."}]}]});
